rows.report:
I added `delete_customer(customer_id)` to `app/customers.py`, but it closes the customer instead of removing the row. I couldn't run or test it.

ADR 0060 (`docs/decisions/0060-a-customer-is-closed-not-deleted.md`) rejects removing the customer row. An earlier `delete_customer` that did `del store.CUSTOMERS[id]` broke the year-end ledger export, because 212 invoices were left pointing at a missing customer. The ADR's Decision says `delete_customer` should set `closed_on` to today's date and keep the row. Commit `308b8bc` had already taken the row-deleting version out.

The function:
- looks up the customer with `store.CUSTOMERS[customer_id]`, which raises `KeyError` for an unknown id, like `get_customer`;
- sets `closed_on` to today's date as an ISO string;
- returns the customer;
- needs `from datetime import date`, which I added at the top of the file.

The Delete button will work, but nothing treats closed customers differently yet. `list_customers` and `get_customer` still return them, and nothing blocks new invoices for them. The ADR's Consequences section says "closing a customer is not built yet", and that is still true. Hiding closed customers from the admin list is a separate change that I haven't made.

The ADR's Consequences also say to take `delete_customer` back out. That seems to conflict with its own Decision, which describes the closing behaviour. I followed the Decision. If you meant for the function to stay out until closing is fully built, tell me and I'll revert it. You may also want to reword that line in the ADR.

